# Localization

- Support `en`, `kk`, and `ru` for user-facing API text.
- Keep localization infrastructure under `app/common/localization`.
- Put locale resolution in `app/common/localization/locale_resolver.py`.
- Put localization lookup behavior in `app/common/localization/service.py`.
- Put translations in `app/common/localization/translations/en.json`, `ru.json`, and `kk.json`.
- Use stable snake_case keys for localization keys, for example `user_not_found` and `invalid_token`. Do not use English sentences as localization keys.
- Call localization with explicit names such as `translate(message_key)`. Do not import or call localization through `_`, `N_`, or other unclear aliases.
- Keep stable localization keys internal; do not expose `message_key` in public API responses unless explicitly requested.
- Map framework validation error types to snake_case localization keys. Do not translate raw English framework messages.
- Select request locale from the `Accept-Language` header and expose that header in OpenAPI for every operation.
- Do not create endpoint-local translation dictionaries. Reuse the shared localization layer.
- Internal logs, SQL strings, enum values, and developer-only runtime diagnostics can remain English unless they are returned to API clients.
